    lessons: [
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:prepare", title: "Give every system a purpose", minutes: 10, summary: "Record the starting configuration and explain what the two controllers will do.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "Start by understanding the services you are about to build. Then record the names, addresses, and dependencies for two controllers in the same domain."), LessonSection("What is Active Directory?", "The shared directory behind domain identities."), LessonSection("A directory for people and computers", "Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) is the Windows Server directory service. It stores objects such as user accounts, computer accounts, and groups, together with their attributes. Administrators use this shared directory to organize identities and manage access consistently."), LessonSection("The domain and the controller", "A domain is a logical collection of directory objects, named here sbd.test. A domain controller (DC) is a server running AD DS that stores and serves that directory. DC01 and DC02 are two servers serving the same domain. A forest contains one or more domains; this build starts with one."), LessonSection("Identity, permissions, and policy", "Authentication checks who is signing in. Authorization determines what that identity may access. Signing in successfully does not grant access to every resource: the destination service still applies its permissions. Groups make it practical to assign access to roles instead of managing every person separately."), LessonSection("Organization and consistent settings", "Organizational units (OUs) organize objects and support delegated administration and Group Policy. Group Policy applies centrally managed settings to users and computers. A global catalog helps find directory objects across a forest; both controllers in this course provide that role."), LessonSection("Remember", "AD DS provides directory and identity services. DNS helps clients find those services. Each has a distinct job."), LessonSection("What is DNS?", "The name and service directory that makes AD discoverable."), LessonSection("Names become reachable addresses", "Domain Name System (DNS) answers queries about names. An A record maps a hostname to an IPv4 address; an AAAA record maps it to an IPv6 address. In this sample, SBD-DC01.sbd.test resolves to 10.0.10.10. DNS also works on an isolated network without internet access."), LessonSection("AD needs service discovery", "Service (SRV) records advertise which servers provide a service. A client uses DNS to discover suitable domain controllers, resolves their hostnames, and contacts a controller for authentication. DNS does not validate the password. A running DC can still be difficult to find if the DNS records or client settings are wrong."), LessonSection("What a zone contains", "A DNS zone holds records for a portion of the namespace. With an AD-integrated zone, records are stored in AD DS and replicated to DNS servers within the configured replication scope. Secure dynamic updates restrict which identities may change records. Check the zone scope and update policy on the completed build."), LessonSection("Two roles, two kinds of checks", "Installing DNS and installing AD DS provide different services, even when they run on the same server. Query each DNS server for SRV and host records, then check directory replication separately. A correct DNS answer proves neither a successful sign-in nor access to a protected resource."), LessonSection("Remember", "Discovery sequence: ask DNS for a domain service → resolve the server name → contact a reachable DC → authenticate."), LessonSection("Prepare two separate VMs", "Use new, patched Windows Server 2025 Desktop Experience installations with console access and the required administrative credentials. Keep the virtual network isolated. Obtain approved media and updates through your established offline process."), LessonSection("Record names and addresses", "Rename the servers SBD-DC01 and SBD-DC02 and restart as needed. Use 10.0.10.10/24 and 10.0.10.11/24 for this sample. No gateway is needed for this same-subnet exercise; the wider network requires its own approved routing plan."), LessonSection("Set the DNS starting point", "For the first DC that will host DNS, use its own static address, 10.0.10.10. Point DC02 at DC01 before promotion. Verify clocks are aligned and document the intended internal time source. Do not add an internet resolver or external forwarder to this isolated network."), LessonSection("Inspect each server before promotion", "hostname\nGet-NetIPConfiguration\nGet-DnsClientServerAddress -AddressFamily IPv4\nw32tm /query /status"), LessonSection("What to look for", "The two names and static addresses match your plan. DC02 points to 10.0.10.10 for DNS. Record actual time-source output; a configured address alone does not prove time synchronization."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "A DNS server address configured on a network adapter does not install DNS. DC01 will not answer DNS queries until its DNS service is installed and running."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record your VM names, OS builds, addresses, DNS settings, and any differences from the sample.")], question: "Before promoting DC02, where should it look for the existing domain's DNS?", answers: ["DC01, once its domain DNS is available", "A public DNS resolver", "Only itself, before DNS is installed"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "DC02 needs to locate the existing directory through DNS. In this sample, DC01 provides that service first.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#prepare", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:dc01", title: "Create the first domain controller", minutes: 10, summary: "Create the sample forest and bring AD DS and DNS online on DC01.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "A new forest establishes the directory's initial boundary. Here, sbd.test is a sample domain, not an existing company domain. Follow this path only for a new forest."), LessonSection("Add the server role", "On DC01, open Server Manager → Manage → Add Roles and Features. Choose the local server and Active Directory Domain Services, including its management tools."), LessonSection("Create the forest", "Select Promote this server to a domain controller, then Add a new forest. Enter sbd.test. For this all-2025 sample, select Windows Server 2025 functional levels. Include DNS and the global catalog. Enter and confirm a unique Directory Services Restore Mode password on the server."), LessonSection("Review and promote", "Review DNS options, the SBD NetBIOS name, paths, and prerequisite results. A parent-zone delegation is not required for this isolated .test forest. Resolve blocking errors, install, and allow the restart. Then inspect the services and domain."), LessonSection("Verify DC01 after the restart", "Get-ADDomain | Select-Object DNSRoot, NetBIOSName\nGet-Service NTDS,DNS,Netlogon\nGet-SmbShare -Name SYSVOL,NETLOGON"), LessonSection("What to look for", "The domain is sbd.test with NetBIOS name SBD. NTDS, DNS, and Netlogon are running, and SYSVOL and NETLOGON shares exist. Investigate missing services or shares before moving on."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "Installing the AD DS role is only the first part. The promotion wizard creates the domain controller; a completed role installation alone is not a completed forest."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record the domain, promotion outcome, service checks, and the file names of your screenshots or saved output. Do not enter passwords.")], question: "What turns an AD DS role installation into a domain controller?", answers: ["Renaming the server", "Completing domain-controller promotion", "Adding a second network adapter"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Promotion configures the directory role for a new forest or an existing domain. Merely adding the role is insufficient.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#dc01", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:dc02", title: "Give the domain a second service point", minutes: 10, summary: "Promote DC02 into the existing domain and inspect the replication relationship.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "A second domain controller gives the domain another live service point. Read why this is a standard availability practice, then add DC02 and verify the result."), LessonSection("Why use two domain controllers?", "Keep identity and DNS services available when one server is unavailable."), LessonSection("Remove the single-server dependency", "With one DC, its outage removes the only live directory service point. With two healthy, reachable controllers, clients can discover a surviving DC for supported operations. This also lets you patch or restart one server while the other continues serving requests. Reconnection may take time; uninterrupted service is not guaranteed."), LessonSection("Both controllers do useful work", "Both writable DCs can process normal directory changes and authentication requests. Replication shares directory changes between them. They are not a primary server and an idle spare. Certain operations still depend on a single FSMO role holder, so role placement and recovery need their own plan."), LessonSection("Make the redundancy real", "Microsoft recommends at least two virtualized DCs per domain on different virtualization hosts. Two VMs on one host share that host as a failure point. Consider shared storage, power, and network dependencies too. Run DNS on both here, verify zone replication, and configure clients to use both internal DNS addresses."), LessonSection("Availability still needs recovery", "Replication can spread an accidental deletion or harmful change to both servers. Keep protected backups and test recovery separately. Two is a starting point for this design, not a universal capacity target: additional sites, users, and recovery needs may require more controllers."), LessonSection("Remember", "Before relying on DC02, verify replication and DNS, review shared dependencies, and test client discovery during an approved maintenance window."), LessonSection("Reach the existing directory", "On DC02, confirm DNS points to 10.0.10.10. Query the domain service records and resolve SBD-DC01.sbd.test. Check network reachability and clock alignment before promotion."), LessonSection("Add a controller to this domain", "Install the AD DS role and management tools. In the promotion wizard choose Add a domain controller to an existing domain, enter sbd.test, and authenticate with an authorized Domain Admin account. Select DNS and global catalog, leave Read only domain controller unselected, and set the DSRM password securely."), LessonSection("Finish and inspect", "Review site placement, replication source, and prerequisite results; install and restart. Keep DC02 using DC01 for DNS until initial inbound replication is healthy. Then document the final internal resolver order for both DCs, retaining a reachable partner and self as appropriate to the design."), LessonSection("Inspect both controllers and replication", "Get-ADDomainController -Filter * |\n  Select-Object HostName, IPv4Address, IsGlobalCatalog\nrepadmin /replsummary\nrepadmin /showrepl SBD-DC02"), LessonSection("What to look for", "Both hostnames appear with the intended addresses and global catalog roles. After initial convergence, replication checks show successful recent attempts and no unresolved replication failures. Inspect errors rather than assuming the restart completed replication."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "Choosing Add a new forest creates a different directory. For this chapter, DC02 joins the domain established by DC01."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Record both controllers, replication status and collection time, plus any failures or follow-up actions.")], question: "A damaging directory change reaches both controllers. What does that illustrate?", answers: ["DC02 should never replicate", "Two controllers guarantee recovery", "Replication and historical recovery are different capabilities"], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: "Both controllers can hold the same unwanted change. Recovery requires protected backups and an appropriate, tested recovery procedure.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#dc02", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:verify", title: "Ask the services to prove they work", minutes: 10, summary: "Query each DNS service explicitly and inspect domain-controller health.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "A service can be running while its configuration is wrong. Query each DNS server by address so the result tells you which server answered. Capture actual results and failures in your evidence record."), LessonSection("What crosses the network?", "The ports a domain member needs to reach its domain controllers."), LessonSection("A flat lab hides the conversation", "Your lab is one flat network, so nothing stands between a client and DC01. In a segmented network, every one of these conversations has to be allowed on purpose. Inside the Network shows them crossing the core switch."), LessonSection("Discovery and sign-in are only the start", "DNS finds a domain controller and Kerberos proves who is signing in. After that, the member reads the directory over LDAP, pulls Group Policy and scripts from SYSVOL over SMB, and uses RPC for NetLogon. Time matters too: Kerberos rejects clocks that drift too far apart."), LessonSection("Remember", "Allowing DNS and Kerberos isn't enough. Finding a domain controller, applying Group Policy, and reading SYSVOL also need LDAP, SMB, and RPC. In the Inside the Network design, rule F03 permits this directory profile for domain members, with LDAP signing and channel binding required."), LessonSection("Query each DNS service", "Run the checks below in an administrative Windows PowerShell session with the server tools available. Query the domain controller locator SRV records separately on DC01 and DC02. SRV means service record."), LessonSection("Check the host records", "Resolve SBD-DC01.sbd.test and SBD-DC02.sbd.test against both DNS servers. Expect 10.0.10.10 and 10.0.10.11 respectively. A locator response does not, by itself, prove that every listed host is reachable."), LessonSection("Review health in context", "Run DCDiag and review each result. In an isolated environment, classify any external-resolution failures against the intended design. The workstation in this scene is a test viewpoint; joining and validating a real Windows client is an optional extension after routing and client prerequisites are ready."), LessonSection("Read-only DNS and health checks", "\$servers = '10.0.10.10','10.0.10.11'\nforeach (\$server in \$servers) {\n  Write-Output \"DNS server: \$server\"\n  Resolve-DnsName '_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.sbd.test' -Type SRV -Server \$server\n  Resolve-DnsName 'SBD-DC01.sbd.test' -Type A -Server \$server\n  Resolve-DnsName 'SBD-DC02.sbd.test' -Type A -Server \$server\n}\ndcdiag /test:DNS /e /v\nrepadmin /replsummary"), LessonSection("What to look for", "Each DNS service returns domain locator records for the configured controllers and the correct host addresses. Review DCDiag findings and replication failures individually. Save output with host, time, and interpretation; do not record an automatic pass because the command ran."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "A successful ping confirms limited IP reachability, not DNS service discovery, directory replication, or authentication."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "For each DNS server, record the observed SRV and A-record results, test time, failures, and what the results establish.")], question: "What does a successful DNS locator query establish?", answers: ["The queried server returned the requested service records", "Every authentication control is satisfied", "The system is authorized to operate"], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: "The query demonstrates a particular DNS response. Reachability, authentication, control effectiveness, and authorization require additional evidence and decisions.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#verify", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide'),
      Lesson(id: "training:dc01-dc02:evidence", title: "Leave a build someone else can understand", minutes: 10, summary: "Assemble your configuration record, test evidence, and remaining work.", sections: [LessonSection("Understand the why", "The useful deliverable is a build you can explain and check again. Your evidence record connects each action with an observed result and identifies what remains unresolved."), LessonSection("Describe the final state", "Record the domain, both controllers, DNS resolver choices, site placement, and actual role holders. Preserve the distinction between the intended design and observed configuration."), LessonSection("Keep a small evidence set", "Store your environment outputs locally with descriptive filenames and collection times. In this companion, write a short summary and the file references; the form does not upload or collect your screenshots, server output, or credentials."), LessonSection("Plan the next check", "List remaining hardening, auditing, backup, recovery, and client-validation work. Assign an owner or next action. Revisit checks after relevant configuration changes. Export the evidence record before moving devices."), LessonSection("Record the current role holders", "Get-ADDomain |\n  Select-Object DNSRoot, PDCEmulator, RIDMaster, InfrastructureMaster\nGet-ADForest |\n  Select-Object RootDomain, SchemaMaster, DomainNamingMaster"), LessonSection("What to look for", "The record identifies the actual role holders and links configuration decisions to dated test results. Open issues remain visible. Lesson completion records learning activity; it is not a system authorization decision."), LessonSection("Avoid this mistake", "A diagram becoming complete does not establish that your VM passed a check. The model tells the lesson story; your observations establish what happened in your environment."), LessonSection("Keep your evidence", "Summarize the final state, reference your evidence files, and list remaining work with a next action or review trigger.")], question: "Which event is a useful reason to revisit the evidence?", answers: ["Only the anniversary of the video", "A relevant configuration change or new finding", "Finishing the animation"], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "Changes and findings can affect prior conclusions. A monitoring plan defines when and how to reassess the relevant security state.", sourceUrl: "https://securebydesigninc.com/training/dc01-dc02/index.html#evidence", sourceLabel: 'Open the complete guide')
    ]),
